Results
All nerve conduction studies, including minimal F wave
latencies, were normal. Soleus muscle H reflex latency and
H/M ratio did not differ from controls. 
Bilateral surface and concentric needle examination of
right and left thoraco-lumbar paraspinal, gluteus maximus,
hamstrings, and quadriceps muscles demonstrated continu-
ous motor unit activity (CMUA) at rest that could not be
suppressed voluntarily. To a lesser extent CMUA was also
present in tibialis anterior and triceps surae muscles. Motor
unit morphology was normal in all muscles. CMUA was
present in agonist and antagonist muscles of thighs and legs
simultaneously, was remarkably reduced by intravenous
diazepam administration (10 mg), and disappeared during
stages 2, 3, and 4 of NREM sleep. Noxious and non-nox-
ious somatosensory stimuli, sudden noises and voluntary
activation, also of the contralateral leg muscles increased
motor unit firing and often provoked spasms, consisting in
large-amplitude, short-duration EMG bursts simultaneous-
ly occurring in antagonist muscle pairs.
Short latency median and posterior tibial nerve SEPs
were bilaterally normal.
In normal subjects no LLR could be evoked at rest. In
our patient 0.8-1 mV amplitude and 50-55 ms latency LLRs
were evoked in proximal muscles “at rest”. LLRs of com-
parable amplitude and latency could be evoked in normal
subjects only when the target muscles were contracting at
about 50% of maximal voluntary contraction (MVC).
Standard EEG was normal. In polygraphic recording of
spontaneous sleep there was a reduction of total sleep time,
preservation of 1-2 sleep stages and reduction of slow-
wave and REM sleep. CMUA decreased after onset of sleep
and disappeared during stages 2, 3, and 4 of NREM sleep.
Myoclonus was not observed in the limbs during sleep.
CCT, peripheral conduction time and amplitude of
EMG responses obtained by cervical and lumbosacral root
magnetic stimulation were normal for all muscles. On TMS
MEP amplitudes, MEP/M ratios and excitability thresholds
were normal for BB and ADM muscles. At rest (Table 1),
excitability thresholds were abnormally reduced for TA and
VM (Fig. 1); MEP amplitudes were increased for TA and
VM; MEP/M ratios, reaching the value of 120% in VM,
probably for the well known difficulty of attaining supra-
maximal stimulation when stimulating the femoral nerve in
the inguinal fossa. In normal subjects, such MEP amplitude
values did not occur even with voluntary contraction at
15%-20% of maximum (normal values, 4.8 ± 2.6 mV and
5.7 ± 2.8 mV for TA and VM, respectively). V oluntary
facilitation further increased TA and VM MEP amplitudes
(the latter reaching 48 mV) (Fig. 1). The silent period was
of normal value in BB and ADM muscles, while absent in
VM and TA muscles.
Treatment and follow-up
A dose of 9 mg/day bromazepam in three divided doses
reduced stiffness and spasms. Higher doses were not toler-
ated because of somnolence. Equivalent doses of diazepam
were also effective. We gradually added tizanidine to bro-
mazepam up to a daily dose of 18 mg. This further
improved stiffness and spasms and the patient could walk
unassisted. EMG examination disclosed reduced but per-
sisting CMUA; LLRs reduced in amplitude. On TMS, TA
and VM excitability thresholds returned to normal values,
MEP amplitudes and MEP/M were still abnormally
increased, and VM and TA SP duration were reduced (Table
1). Tizanidine was discontinued and baclofen was gradual-
ly added to bromazepam up to 75 mg/day in three divided
doses. There was marked improvement. Stiffness and
spasms disappeared, CMUA was not detected in EMG
